 "genre_note": "Cluster c17 is 20 Sporting News sports articles in five distinct genres, and the genre \u2014 not the word count \u2014 drove every boundary.\n\nBUILDING-BLOCK DISTRIBUTION. As expected for news, empirical_observation dominates (~70 of 118 notes) and concept is second (~20). argument (~22) and counter_argument (~5) are concentrated in exactly two places: the Michigan sign-stealing scandal, where the whole story IS a contested claim (Big Ten's head-coach-responsibility doctrine vs. Michigan's due-process letter, Warde Manuel's rebuttal, the \"everyone steals signs\" counterclaim), and the betting-advice material, where every pick is a claim with grounds. procedure appears only in the evergreen betting how-to guides (line shopping steps, reading prop odds, sportsbook prop rules, SuperDraft parlay mechanics). model appears three times, only where the source genuinely explains a mechanism: how sportsbooks set and move lines, why moneyline odds change, and the Copa America qualification path (a conditional chain: win the tie -> Copa berth + semifinals; lose -> a playoff against a third team). hypothesis DOES NOT OCCUR and I manufactured none. Betting predictions look superficially like hypotheses but are presented as recommendations with supporting evidence, so they are argument. navigation was not produced.\n\nTOPICAL COHERENCE OVER SIZE. Three decisions are worth flagging. (1) The five live blogs (doc_0037, doc_0144, doc_0294, doc_0157, doc_0232) are single-subject documents but 2.2k-4.5k words, so I split them at quarter/half boundaries \u2014 a real sub-topic boundary in a game narrative, not a word count \u2014 plus separate notes for the recap, pregame/team news, and broadcast details. (2) doc_0119 (NFL power rankings) is the roundup hazard the brief warns about: 32 team blurbs. Merging them would produce a note retrieved for every NFL team that answers nothing, so I split it into four ranked bands of eight. (3) Conversely, doc_0061's seven sport-by-sport moneyline examples ARE one subject (worked examples of the same calculation) and stayed one note.\n\nSHARED ENTITY NOTES. Four documents cover the Michigan scandal, so shared notes draw blocks from several: the Big Ten suspension note pulls doc_0079 and doc_0180; the TRO-litigation note pulls doc_0180, doc_0079 and doc_0353; the scandal overview and the Stalions note each span two documents; Sherrone Moore's tenure spans doc_0079 and doc_0353. The two soccer legs stayed separate because they are separate matches with separate results, but the qualification-path note is the cross-leg hinge.\n\nWHAT I DROPPED, AND WHAT I RESCUED. Dropped: article titles, dated sub-headers in the two timeline/roundup documents, \"MORE:\"/\"See more:\"/\"JUMP TO:\" cross-links, the repeated \"QBs | RBs | WRs | TEs\" rankings navigation strips, the Draft Sharks and SuperDraft promo blocks, embed furniture (\"pic.twitter.com/...\", \"\ud83d\udcfa: #CHIvsMIN on ESPN/ABC\"), and contentless hype captions (\"Two drives. Two touchdowns.\", \"Strap in folks!\"). Rescued on inspection: several blocks that look like chrome carry the scope condition on their own document's headline. doc_0180 [66]-[67] reads like boilerplate but states that the Big Ten could NOT prove Harbaugh knew about the scheme \u2014 the exact opposite of what \"Harbaugh suspended for sign-stealing\" implies, and the pivot of the whole dispute; it is its own note. doc_0180 [124] quietly says the sign-decoding Michigan alleges of Ohio State, Purdue and Rutgers is NOT the violation \u2014 in-person scouting is. doc_0296 [8]/[11]/[13]/[16] look like list headers but ARE the picks. doc_0119's \"1. Philadelphia Eagles 8-1 (previous week: 1)\" lines are data rows carrying rank and record, not headers.\n\nCOVERAGE. Seventeen of 20 documents land at 91-97% of words assigned. Three land lower and the reason is promotional density, not compression: doc_0114 (~85%) is a rankings page carrying five separate cross-link strips plus a Draft Sharks sales block; doc_0288 (~87%) is an injury page with a nav strip between every player; doc_0180 (~92%) loses 49 dated sub-headers and date lines that a timeline format requires but that duplicate the prose beneath them. No (doc, block) pair is assigned to two notes; every block is either assigned exactly once or listed in dropped."
